Real-World Implementation Pattern
class LLMEnhancedTopicAgent:
    def __init__(self, topic, llm_client):
        self.topic = topic
        self.llm = llm_client

    def process_raw_information(self, raw_data):
        # Step 1: Context Understanding
        context = self.llm.complete(
            f"Analyze this data about {self.topic} and explain what's really being discussed",
            raw_data
        )

        # Step 2: Relevance Filtering
        filtered = self.llm.complete(
            f"Score each item 1-10 for relevance to {self.topic} for our {self.audience}",
            raw_data,
            context
        )

        # Step 3: Quality Assessment
        quality_checked = self.llm.complete(
            "Assess credibility of sources, flag any concerns",
            filtered
        )

        # Step 4: Noise Reduction
        clean_insights = self.llm.complete(
            "Extract only substantive insights, remove noise",
            quality_checked
        )

        # Step 5: Evolution Tracking
        trends = self.llm.complete(
            "Compare to our last analysis, what's changed?",
            clean_insights,
            self.previous_analysis
        )

        return {
            'context': context,
            'key_insights': clean_insights,
            'evolution': trends,
            'high_quality_sources': quality_checked.top_sources
        }

The Practical Reality
What LLMs Do Well:

Understanding messy human communication
Connecting disparate pieces of information
Filtering obvious junk
Explaining complex relationships
Identifying patterns in text

What You Still Need:

Structured data extraction (dates, numbers, entities)
Real-time verification (is this still true?)
Quantitative analysis (statistical trends)
Source discovery (finding new places to look)
Deduplication at scale (exact matching)

Optimal Hybrid Approach
class HybridIntelligenceAgent:
    def process_topic(self, topic, raw_data):
        # Algorithmic: Deduplication, entity extraction
        structured = self.extract_structured_data(raw_data)

        # LLM: Context and understanding
        analysis = self.llm_analyze(structured)

        # Algorithmic: Temporal organization
        timeline = self.create_timeline(structured)

        # LLM: Insight generation
        insights = self.llm_generate_insights(analysis, timeline)

        # Algorithmic: Store and index
        self.store_findings(insights)

        # LLM: Natural language report
        report = self.llm_create_report(insights)

        return report


The Bottom Line
LLMs can handle 70-80% of the intelligence work, especially:

Making sense of messy data
Understanding context
Filtering relevance
Reducing noise
Generating insights

You still need traditional code for:

Data collection
Temporal tracking
Quantitative analysis
Storage/retrieval
Verification

The key is using LLMs for what they're brilliant at (understanding language and context) while using traditional programming for structure, scale, and precision.

